Structs again, and multivalued expressions
==========================================

Oh no, more structs!
--------------------

So, I had a bit of a blank in my head during implementation, when I was 
dealing with the `ref` keyword, which is meant to signify that a struct 
as single return value of a method must be returned by reference, 
instead of by value.

It had been bothered by the need to add a keyword at the time, and now 
that I think about it again, I do realize that it may not be necessary 
at all. Indeed, the point was that since structs are normally returned 
by value, it was impossible to modify a struct contained in another 
object or container:

	someobject.somestruct.structmember = 1
	structarray[20].structmember = 2

is really:

	temp = someobject.somestruct	-- copy!
	temp.structmember = 1			-- modify local value
	
	temp2 = structarray[20]			-- copy!
	temp2.structmember = 2			-- modify local value

And since that was slightly counterintuitive, especially since it works 
differently in about any other language with structs, I decided to add 
the possibility of returning a single struct by reference, so that an 
immediate call to a setter would modify it in place.

But, the other side of the coin is that it adds one keyword, and one 
more non trivial concept to explain.

What about simplifying to:

1.	structs are always copied at assignment (including to function 
	arguments and from return values)
2.	local structs are returned by value
3.	a struct reference is returned as is

Since the automatic getter for a struct member (and the indexing of an 
array) returns it by reference, rule three creates a nice transitivity 
that basically means "non-local structs are returned by reference".

No one needs to know what happens behind the scenes. What happens is the 
expected behavior: if it's a member or an element of an array, you can 
modify it in place. If it was a local, you can also modify it in place, 
but it will be very useless if you don't retrieve the value somewhere 
else.

This means:

	blah = MyStruct
	
	foo = blah			-- copied
	someMethod(foo)		-- copied to a local in the method
	obj.member = foo	-- copied to member
	bar = obj.member	-- "obj.member" is a reference, but it's 
						-- assigned, therefore copied, to "bar"
	obj.member.a = 1	-- "obj.member" is a reference, so the struct is 
						-- modified in place
	bar = obj.makevalue	-- copy the return value
	obj.makevalue.a = 1	-- if that method returned a local, this call is 
						-- very useless, but that's really your fault...

The compiler internally discriminate methods, and keeps track of what is 
a reference and what is a value. Anyway, the only thing you can do with 
a struct reference is send it a message, return it as is, or copy the 
pointed value to a temporary local. It does require the code generation 
to handle the cases differently, obviously, but it ends up being 
equivalent to filling in the `ref` keyword automatically in 
declarations.

Pros:

1.	"returning a struct" always acts as expected, in particular for 
	non-local modification
2.	less typing for the same meaning: since the compiler can decide 
	itself how to act, no need for a redundant `ref` keyword that 
	requires more analysis, and can lead to user errors. It's the 
	classic "you have forgotten a ; here" that C/C++ compilers tell you. 
	Well, if you know where I should have put one, maybe you could have 
	put it for me, or maybe the language didn't need one in the first 
	place.

Cons:

1.	too much hidden behavior could be evil, since it makes it less easy 
	to understand
2.	non consistent behavior with the same syntax: sometimes it's this, 
	sometimes it's that. But then again it really comes down to whether 
	you return a local or not, so it's not rocket science either.

In any case, and it will be clearly stated in the reference, structures 
are merely there for efficiency reasons, and they are a more advanced 
feature of the language, that require a bit of insight to use correctly.

Anything structures can do, objects could do to, but sometimes at the 
price of execution speed and memory use.

I also just thought about this, but structures have the funny 
possibility of having themselves as member, and therefore be of infinite 
size. That's one more thing the analyzer will have to check.

Using multiple return values
----------------------------

This little structure chat made me think about something I hadn't 
thought about before. So far, I always envisioned multiple return values 
to be used directly in assignments:

	a,b,c = someMethod()
	otherMethod(someMethod())	-- that's three arguments right there

and also spread out in array-like object constructors:

	foo = [someMethod()]	-- three elements, most likely of the same 
							-- type

But I haven't though about sending messages to multiple values:

	someMethod.doTheLulz	-- someMethod has 3 return values!

What do we do here?

-	Do we send the message to the first return value, and ignore the 
	others?
-	Do we send the same message to all return values? And if so, should 
	we gather all return values of all calls into one big multi-valued 
	expression? Like here if `doTheLulz` returns 2 values, the whole 
	expression would have 6-valued? Obviously that can work only 
	if all return values are of the same type, or at least if they 
	answer to the same method. Which might be a rare case. How often 
	would a method return many values that have an equivalent status, 
	and that would be treated the same way? When that is the case, 
	returning an array is probably the way to go.
-	Do we just complain about it and blame the user for being lame?

Likewise, can we ignore some or all return values in assignment?

	a,b = someMethod	-- someMethod returns 3 values
	foobar(someMethod)	-- foobar takes in only 1 value
	doSomething			-- doSomething returns 2 values

That's where functional and imperative programming meet and greet. Some 
methods are meant to take in data, compute stuff, and return a result. 
Some have mostly side-effects: modify the state of memory somewhere. 
Most do both.

And this is even accentuated by my intentional choice of having the 
parentheses optional for methods without arguments:

	obj.blah

Is `blah` a member? Is it a computation that returns something? Does it 
do something silently and return nothing? I had planned to have the 
compiler warn about ignoring the return value of non-void methods, to 
avoid mistakes like:

	method foo()
		obj.getSomething	-- hey! you forgot to take your something!
	end

But I can also imagine lots of methods with side effects that also 
return an additional information about what happened, and that can be 
safely ignored. For instance, a stream could return itself so that you 
can chain calls, like in C++:

	std::cout << "Foo " << somevar << " and bar" << std::endl;

But the return value of the last call will definitely be ignored.

And if we can ignore one can we ignore more? Select just what we want. 
In Lua, many pattern matching functions return lots of information, like 
the match, its position, and then optional captures. If we still want 
only the information of whether there was a match, we can perfectly do:

	if text:match("can you find me?") then
		-- blah blah
	end

Here, the boolean evaluation is done only on the first argument, which 
will be `nil` if the substring was not found. This is definitely an 
idiom I want in sool.

So, mmm, yes. If boolean evaluation discards all but the first return 
value, maybe that should happen as well for a direct method call:

	-- call print on the first result, which is the match
	somestring.match("hello %w+").print

Lua does the discarding also when multi-valued expressions appear within 
expression lists:

	a,b,c = 1,somefunction(),2

Whatever the number of return values of `somefunction`, only the first 
one gets assigned to `b`. I don't think I want that. I want multiple 
values to be unfolded as expected and assigned to what's needed:

	a,b,c,d,e = 1,returnthreevalues(),2
	iTakeThreeArgs(1,iReturnTwoValues())

We've talking about discarding values, what about missing ones?

	a,b,c,d = iReturnTwoValues()

That stands in assignments (and return statements and method calls, 
which have the same semantics). In Lua, it's simple, anything missing 
gets `nil`.

	iTakeThreeArgs(1)	-- second and third will be nil
	return 1			-- if the caller expects 3 things, the two 
						-- others will be nil

That is convenient for default arguments and typing less. That's also 
cool if you don't want to type `return nil` all the time. It's implied. 
In sool, where there is proper method overloading, that would just lead 
to lots of ambiguity in selecting which method to call, just like in 
C++.

Furthermore, we don't really have a proper `nil` in sool. The "default" 
or "invalid" or "uninitialized" value is not coherently defined for all 
types. There is `nil` for objects, `nan` for numbers. Arguably, 0 for 
blobs. Booleans don't have any, and structs are even a different story 
altogether.

So that sounds difficult. You can provide too many values, and they will 
be silently discarded, but you always need to give at least as many as 
are required.
